WEBVTT

1.1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:08.370
Before assessing how firms respond to sanctions, the first question is
which firms remain visible in the data.

2.1
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:21.540
The Russian Financial Statements Database, or RFSD, records annual
financial statements. A firm can remain on the business register in a
year when no statement for that firm appears in the database.

3.1
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:33.070
Fewer statements can still mean a higher share when fewer firms are
counted. Statement counts are therefore shown alongside the shares.

4.1
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.390
These maps show statement availability at registered addresses.

5.1
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:50.590
Sanctions expanded after Russia’s annexation of Crimea in 2014 and again
as the war in Ukraine escalated in 2022.

5.2
00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:03.310
These hundred-kilometre cells compare 2021 with 2024, showing local
increases and decreases in the share of firms with statements.

5.3
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.270
That share rose overall among firms with mapped addresses.

6.1
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.790
Aeroflot, Russia’s national carrier with partial government ownership,
contrasts with a nearby private dental practice selected for its
complete statement history.

6.2
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.790
The wider analysis examines how statement availability differs by
ownership and firm size.

7.1
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.390
Aeroflot has no statement here for 2022, but does have one for 2023.

8.1
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:55.310
Each firm’s average real revenue from 2019 to 2021 is set to one
hundred. A value of eighty means the firm’s real revenue was twenty
percent below that average.

9.1
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:12.150
Business centres can house firms in separate offices at one street
address. Matching only the street can also put different addresses at
the same point. Larger marks can therefore represent several businesses.

10.1
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:24.150
Hollow red marks show firms matched to at least one of the five lists
when their statements are missing.

11.1
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:35.910
Revenue colours compare changes with the median for mapped firms with
usable revenue records and no match on any of the five lists.

11.2
00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.340
A firm can be above that median even while its revenue falls.

12.1
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:52.710
Missing statements limit which firms can be included in financial
analysis. The next comparisons show which firms are more likely to be
left out.

13.1
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:05.830
From 2021 to 2024, statement availability rose among private firms and
fell among state-related firms, with the decline concentrated among
state and municipal enterprises.

14.1
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:15.030
Firms without a statement were more likely to be removed the next year.
Tracking removal separately shows which firms remain registered.

15.1
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:30.870
Among firms with 2021 accounts that stayed registered, 95 percent of the
largest firms had a statement again in 2022, compared with 84 percent of
the smallest.

16.1
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.130
The study matches firms separately to five lists. A firm can match more
than one.

16.2
00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:45.480
The chart compares firms matched only to the sanctions list or only to
the Russian government’s strategic list with firms matched to none of
the five.

17.1
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:56.450
Chapter Three asks whether cash and inventories held in 2021 predicted
revenue and operating margins during 2022 to 2024.
